UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 22, Lesson 2: Services in our community', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place (rural/urban), so geographers and citizens ask who is served and who might miss out.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy might a rural community need different transport services than an inner-city suburb?\n\nFEEDBACK\nGreater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links.\n\nWATCH FOR\nAssuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMap eight local services. Note two access challenges and one improvement idea. Cite how you found each service location.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place (rural/urban), so geographers and citizens ask who is served and who might miss out.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Map eight local services. Note two access challenges and one improvement idea. Cite how you found each service location. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links. Reasoning: I used community by attending to service rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs. Correction: Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links. Why: the correction restores accurate use of community.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating community on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with community, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links. This matches the lesson skill for community.","options":["Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links."],"question":"Why might a rural community need different transport services than an inner-city suburb?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs.","Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place (rural/urban), so geographers and citizens ask who…","Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links.","Careful use of service with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising community?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named service, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the service used."],"question":"A learner is practising community. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links. uses sources and places appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work.","Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links.","Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about community?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about community.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about service?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example map: clinic, school, fire station marked. Discussion: which services are farther for some families?","Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs.","Treat a missing fact as if it were recorded in a source.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs.","Map eight local services. Note two access challenges and one improvement idea. Cite how you found each service location.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of community?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating community on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links. Reasoning: I used community by attending to service rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with community, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs. Correction: Greater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links. Why: the correction restores accurate use of community.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y4-w22-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise community.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Civics and Citizenship","success_criteria":["I can use community with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify service and explain why it matters.","I can practise access independently on a new prompt.","I can notice need needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place (rural/urban), so geographers and citizens ask who is served and who might miss out. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no evidence.","Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place (rural/urban), so geographers and citizens ask who is served and who might miss out."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about community?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample map: clinic, school, fire station marked. Discussion: which services are farther for some families?\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy might a rural community need different transport services than an inner-city suburb?\n\nFEEDBACK\nGreater distances / fewer frequent buses — accept reasoned geography links.","explain":"Community services meet needs such as health, learning, safety and recreation. Access can differ by place (rural/urban), so geographers and citizens ask who is served and who might miss out.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Assuming everyone has the same access ignores distance and disability needs. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Community services access.","caption":"Not equal for all.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Access</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">who can reach</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Rural / urban</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">different distances</text></g></svg>"}],